Mini Browser

[[MBIMG0]]

Welcome to Mini Browser, a compact web browser for the
WHY2025 badge. It is designed to explore lightweight and
server-rendered websites while staying small enough to run
comfortably on the badge. 

It supports links, bookmarks, Back/Forward history, simple
GET and POST forms, bounded cookie support, PNG/JPEG/GIF
images, inline image rendering, multiple display modes,
56,352 Unicode glyphs, word-aware wrapping, RTL/Bidi text,
Arabic shaping, monochrome emoji, real bold text, HTTP/TLS
page information and full-page screenshots.

The current version is Mini Browser 4.4. It adds find in
page, a simplified view for long articles, text zoom, a
focus ring for links and fields, pages saved for offline
reading, sharing a page as a QR code, a page information
screen with a lock for secure pages, and a built-in help
page: press WHY+/ (the /? key) to see every key. 

Version 4.3 brought loading progress with a stop key
(Esc), clear error pages, an instant Back/Forward cache,
compressed pages, cookies that stay after a restart, a 2
MB disk cache, downloads, and images that load after the
text so you can start reading at once. Version 4.1 added
the omnibox (one bar for addresses and searches, with
suggestions), a history that survives a restart, and up to
five tabs. 

= Search the small web
[1] q: 
[2] [Search Wiby]

Wiby is a search engine focused on simple, lightweight
websites and is a good match for Mini Browser. 


= Search with Google
[3] q: 
[4] [Search Google]

Google can also be searched directly from Mini Browser.
Search result pages may be more complex than Wiby because
Google is designed for modern web browsers. 


= Recommended
* [5]Wiby - search the classic web
* [6]Marginalia Search - lightweight search
* [7]FrogFind - search and simplify pages
* [8]Hacker News - technology news and discussion
* [9]NPR Text - text-oriented news
* [10]TEXTFILES.COM - historic text files
* [11]curl - curl project website
* [12]ifconfig.co - network information

= How to browse
Links and form controls have numbers. Type a number and
press Enter to activate it. Use Up and Down to scroll;
hold an arrow for continuous scrolling. J and K also
scroll one line at a time. 
Press WHY+L and type a web address or just some words. An
address such as wiby.me opens directly; words such as
esp32 badge become a Google search. While you type,
matching bookmarks and visited pages appear under the bar:
use Down and Up to pick one and Enter to open it, or Esc
to cancel. 
WHY+T opens a new tab with your bookmarks and recent
pages. WHY+Tab or WHY+1 to WHY+5 switch tabs, WHY+A shows
all tabs and WHY+W closes one. 
While a page loads, the bar shows its progress; press Esc
to stop. If a page cannot be loaded, an error page tells
you why; press R or Enter to try again. The text appears
first and the images follow; the bar shows [img 2/5] while
they arrive. 
Press WHY+F to find a word on the page: matches light up
while you type, n and Shift+N jump to the next and
previous match, Esc turns them off. Tab and Shift+Tab move
a ring over the links and fields, Enter opens the one with
the ring. On a long article a chip offers a simplified
view: press WHY+V to read just the article, without menus
and sidebars. WHY+= and WHY+- make the text bigger or
smaller, WHY+0 returns to 100%. 
WHY+P saves the text of a page to read later without a
network; WHY+D lists your saved pages and downloads. WHY+U
shows a QR code of the page address, so you can open it on
your phone. WHY+I shows whether the connection is secure,
how long the page took and which cookies the site set.
Forgot a key? Press WHY+/ for help. 

= Browser keys
* WHY+/ - help with all the keys
* WHY+L - omnibox: type an address or a search
* WHY+E - enter a new URL
* WHY+C - edit the current URL
* WHY+H - home
* WHY+R - reload
* WHY+B - back
* WHY+G - forward
* Esc - stop loading
* WHY+T - new tab
* WHY+W - close tab
* WHY+Tab - next tab
* WHY+1 ... WHY+5 - go to tab 1 to 5
* WHY+A - tab overview
* WHY+F - find in page (n next, Shift+N previous)
* Tab / Shift+Tab - next / previous link or field
* WHY+V - simplified view
* WHY+= / WHY+- / WHY+0 - bigger / smaller / normal text
* WHY+K - add or remove bookmark
* WHY+M - bookmarks
* WHY+Y - history of visited pages
* WHY+P - save the page for offline reading
* WHY+D - downloads and saved pages
* WHY+U - share the page as a QR code
* WHY+I - page information
* WHY+X - clear: history (on the history page), downloads
and saved pages (on the downloads page), cookies and cache
(on page information)
* WHY+O - display mode options
* WHY+S - capture the visible 716x716 viewport
* WHY+Z - capture the complete rendered page
* WHY+Q - quit

= Screenshots
Press WHY+S to capture the visible 716x716 Mini Browser
view, or WHY+Z to capture the complete rendered page.
Screenshots are transmitted over USB serial. The
repository includes badge_screenshot.py, a standalone
macOS/Linux receiver that validates the CRC/FEC-protected
transfer and saves it as a PNG. 

./badge_screenshot.py /dev/cu.wchusbserial10
./badge_screenshot.py /dev/cu.wchusbserial10 --full-page

Physical WHY+S and WHY+Z screenshots do not require custom
firmware. An optional customized WHY2025 firmware adds
host-to-badge keyboard control, so a computer can operate
Mini Browser remotely. It also includes scripts for
automated browser tests and automatic screenshots. 
[13] Customized WHY2025 firmware and test tools  

= About Mini Browser
Mini Browser combines a built-in ASCII font with an
external GNU Unifont-based MBCJ font containing 56,352
usable glyphs across 101 ranges. Text wrapping preserves
complete words where possible, while CJK and overlong
tokens safely fall back to glyph wrapping. Hebrew and
Arabic are rendered right-to-left, with contextual Arabic
shaping. HTML b and strong text are rendered as genuine
bold text. 
Unordered list markers deliberately remain ASCII *
characters, so basic list rendering does not depend on the
external Unicode font. 
Mini Browser intentionally has no JavaScript engine, CSS
layout engine or full HTML DOM. It focuses on ordinary
server-rendered HTML, text content, links, PNG, JPEG and
GIF images and simple GET and POST forms. Pages that need
JavaScript often still read well in the simplified view
(WHY+V). 
